  for i in range(w, len(series)):\n            window = series[i - w:i]\n            mean = sum(window) / w\n            var = sum((v - mean) ** 2 for v in window) / w\n            sigma = math.sqrt(var)\n            if sigma > 0 and abs(series[i] - mean) > z * sigma:\n                flagged.append(i)\n        return flagged\n","approach":"**Model the recent past as a Gaussian and flag points that are improbable under it.** The trailing window estimates the local mean and spread; a value more than `z` standard deviations away is an outlier at that moment. Excluding the current value from the window matters: including it would inflate `sigma` and hide the very spike you want to catch. The `sigma = 0` rule is a deliberate design choice: a perfectly flat history gives no scale, so any deviation would be infinitely many standard deviations away; real systems add a floor to `sigma` or require a minimum spread instead.\n\nThe direct implementation recomputes each window in O(w). Rolling sums of `x` and `x^2` bring it to O(n), but `E[x^2] - E[x]^2` loses precision when the mean is large relative to the spread, so prefer Welford's algorithm or periodically recompute from scratch in production.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** How do you prevent a detected spike from poisoning the next `w` windows? (skip flagged values when updating the statistics, or use a robust median/MAD estimator). How do you handle trends and seasonality, which this method flags as anomalies? (differencing, exponentially weighted statistics, seasonal baselines). How do you pick `w` and `z`, and what is the trade-off between detection delay and false positives?","complexity":{"time":"O(n * w), or O(n) with rolling sums","space":"O(1) extra beyond the output"},"similar":["reservoir-sample-deterministic","sliding-window-maximum"],"number":196,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def detectAnomalies(self, series: List[float], w: int, z: float) -> List[int]:\n        pass\n"}